  "body": "## Principles\n- Graduate school is worth it when a target role needs the degree, or when the person wants research. Otherwise a job or a cheaper credential may teach more.\n- Cost, time, funding and the specific program matter more than the general idea.\n- Work first, then school, is a real option and often improves the application.\n## Steps\n- Write the target role and check ten real postings for whether the degree is required or helpful.\n- Ask three people in the role about the degree's actual value to them.\n- Compare the total cost, funding, time, and the job outcomes the program states itself, treating those as claims to check.\n- Consider working a year or two while studying part-time or applying later.\n- Decide on a date and what evidence would change the decision.\n## Ask\n- Ask which role they want in five years.\n## Do not claim\n- Do not state tuition, salary gain, rankings or admission odds."
