  "body": "## Principles\n- A cold email is short, specific to the reader, and asks for something small. Three to five sentences, readable on a phone.\n- The structure: why this person, who I am in one line, proof in one line, a small clear ask, an easy out.\n- Specific beats flattering. Mention one real thing: a project, a post, a paper, a product decision.\n- Fewer, better messages beat blasts. Send to people who can decide or know who does.\n## Steps\n- Subject line: plain and specific, such as a question about their work or a short request, not a clever phrase.\n- First line: the real observation about their work.\n- Second line: who the person is and one proof, with a link.\n- Ask: fifteen minutes this week, or a small task they could hand off.\n- Close: thanks and an easy out (\"no problem if the timing is wrong\").\n- Follow up once after about a week with something new, like a short result or an answer to something they said; then stop.\n- Track in a sheet: name, date, subject, reply, next date.\nA shape that works, with [brackets] for facts you do not have: \"Hi [name], I read [specific thing] and liked [what]. I'm a [year] studying [major] and I [one line of proof, link]. Would you have fifteen minutes this week for a call about [one narrow question]? Thanks either way, [name]\"\n## Ask\n- Ask who the person wants to reach, so the message can be written to them.\n## Do not claim\n- Do not guess a person's email address or say a reply is likely.\n- Do not send for the person; they approve and send the exact text."
