  "body": "## Principles\n- The final year is a full-time search with a deadline: the graduation date. Time is the scarce resource, so favor channels with the shortest path to an interview.\n- Many full-time roles for new graduates are hired on a rolling basis, so a late start is recoverable, but every month of waiting narrows options.\n- Offers come from people more often than from portals: referrals, alumni, professors, internship managers.\n## Steps\n- Write down the target role titles (two or three) and the target places; every action serves those.\n- Message past internship managers, professors and alumni in the field: say what role the person wants and ask for one name or one introduction.\n- Apply in small batches of well-matched roles with a tailored first paragraph, rather than hundreds of blind forms.\n- Use the school: career center resume review, alumni database, employer events, and any job board the school runs.\n- Set the weekly numbers: five targeted applications, five human messages, two follow-ups, one practice interview.\n- Line up a bridge plan if the search runs long: contract work, a small employer, or a research assistant role.\n## Ask\n- Ask for the graduation timing and the target role if either is missing.\n## Do not claim\n- Do not cite hiring volumes or pay for new graduates."
